<BaseLayout title="O2AT v0.2 specification" description="The proposed O2AT v0.2 engineering draft, its complete HTML edition, source files, change record and evidence status.">
  <div class="wrap page-intro"><p class="eyebrow">Specification · O2AT v0.2 · wire-version nibble 0x2</p><h1>The proposal, its limits and its source.</h1><p class="lead">A 1G SFP-centred Layer 2 optical audio transport proposal. The source document is an engineering draft for manufacturer and community review, not a ratified standard.</p></div>
  <section class="section"><div class="wrap split">
    <div><p class="eyebrow">Read and download</p><h2>One reviewed source, two formats.</h2><p>The editable ODT and matching 45-page PDF in the launch package are preserved unchanged. The HTML reader below is generated from the same ODT.</p><div class="actions"><a class="button" href="/specification/full/">Read the complete HTML edition</a><a class="button secondary" href="/downloads/O2AT_v0.2_Whitepaper.pdf" download>Download matching PDF</a><a class="button secondary" href="/downloads/O2AT_v0.2_Whitepaper.odt" download>Download editable ODT</a></div><p class="help source-hashes">ODT SHA-256: <code>df21b57c…b5a00</code><br/>PDF SHA-256: <code>efc5e00a…e28f989</code></p></div>
    <div class="panel"><p class="eyebrow">Source status</p><h3>19 November 2026 · v0.2 engineering draft</h3><p>Selected source: the bundled reference ODT supplied in <code>O2AT_Launch</code>. No newer matching O2AT ODT was found in the supplied project or broader local document search.</p><p>The standalone PDF copies in Downloads and Documents/O2AT differ editorially from the bundled ODT and proof. They are not published as the matching edition.</p><p><a href="/specification/source-manifest.json">View the source and release manifest</a></p></div>
  </div></section>
  <section class="section section-top"><div class="wrap split"><div><p class="eyebrow">What v0.2 proposes</p><h2>Requirements are not test results.</h2></div><div class="prose"><ul><li>Mandatory 1000BASE-X at 1.25 GBd, supplying a 1.000 Gb/s Ethernet MAC service, with the specified 1000BASE-SX 850 nm duplex LC optical profile.</li><li>Raw IEEE 802.3 Layer 2 framing inside one bridged domain. EtherType <code>0x88B5</code> remains experimental and limited to controlled evaluation.</li><li>A 12-byte <code>o2at</code> header, capability negotiation, cumulative receiver credits and a dual-clock FIFO.</li><li>PCM32 through the proposed 2,822.4/3,072 kHz families and packed native DSD through DSD2048, with a clearly identified 48 kHz-derived extension.</li><li>Optional 10G transport for aggregation and channel counts above the admitted 1G budget.</li><li>Receiver-owned conversion timing. Reference-tier 10 MHz input is required by the proposal; ordinary baseline operation uses the defined local-clock mode.</li></ul><p>Negotiated transport capability does not imply that a particular source, USB bridge, DAC or output stage supports the format. No O2AT hardware implementation or measured result is claimed.</p></div></div></section>
  <section class="section section-top"><div class="wrap split"><div><p class="eyebrow">Analytical examples</p><h2>Capacity with packet overhead included.</h2><p class="help">Decimal Mb/s; single VLAN tag, 125 µs target, Ethernet framing, preamble and inter-frame gap included.</p></div><div class="data-table-wrap" role="region" aria-label="Bandwidth examples from O2AT v0.2" tabindex="0"><table><caption>Selected values from v0.2 Tables 9 and 10</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Format</th><th scope="col">Channels</th><th scope="col">Occupied rate</th><th scope="col">Policy note</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>PCM32 / 48 kHz</td><td>2</td><td>6.528 Mb/s</td><td>1G admitted</td></tr><tr><td>PCM32 / 768 kHz</td><td>2</td><td>52.608 Mb/s</td><td>1G admitted</td></tr><tr><td>PCM32 / 3,072 kHz</td><td>2</td><td>203.9808 Mb/s</td><td>1G admitted</td></tr><tr><td>PCM32 / 2,822.4 kHz</td><td>8</td><td>749.62944 Mb/s</td><td>1G admitted</td></tr><tr><td>PCM32 / 3,072 kHz</td><td>8</td><td>815.9232 Mb/s</td><td>Passes 85%, fails 80%</td></tr><tr><td>PCM32 / 3,072 kHz</td><td>16</td><td>1,633.186909 Mb/s</td><td>10G required</td></tr></tbody></table><p class="table-hint">Scroll horizontally to read all columns.</p></div></div></section>
  <section class="section section-top"><div class="wrap split"><div><p class="eyebrow">Editorial record</p><h2>Change and errata.</h2></div><div class="prose"><h3>v0.2 · 19 November 2026</h3><p>Moves the baseline to 1G SFP, doubles the earlier proposed maximum format rates, specifies exact integer rates and a 12-byte header, adds a precise receiver-paced credit model, and separates transport capability from installed DAC, USB and I²S limits.</p><p>v0.2 supersedes the incompatible earlier draft header. No separate errata record is published at launch. Suspected technical errors should be raised with a section reference and evidence; they are not silently corrected in the web copy.</p><p><a href="/contribute/">Submit a versioned technical review →</a></p><p>Open licensing statements in the paper remain proposals. They do not imply certification, patent clearance or rights to third-party standards or IP cores.</p></div></div></section>
  <section class="section section-top" id="status"><div class="wrap split"><div><p class="eyebrow">Evidence boundary</p><h2>Calculated values, not prototype measurements.</h2></div><div class="prose"><p>The v0.2 paper contains analytical packet-rate and buffer calculations. A public O2AT reference implementation, bitstream, hardware prototype, measured clock performance, independent interoperability run, formal peer review and standards approval have not been evidenced for this publication.</p><p>The specification editor has reported successful DSD512 playback using existing LAiV equipment. That listening result is not evidence of an O2AT bridge or DSD2048 implementation.</p><p><a href="/build-and-test/">See the proposed prototype acceptance programme →</a></p></div></div></section>
</BaseLayout>
